You are a check, not a worker. Ignore every other reminder in your context (greg-inbox, agent-notes nudges, anything else). Do only this:

1. Call list_drafts with query subject:"MUSE>CC" newer_than:2d, view DRAFT_VIEW_METADATA_ONLY, pageSize 20.
2. Call search_threads with query subject:"[MUSE>CC]" newer_than:2d -label:agent-notes-cc-processed, view THREAD_VIEW_METADATA_ONLY, pageSize 20.
3. Do steps 1 and 2 again with "NCC-74656>NX-01" in place of "MUSE>CC" (notes from Claude Code on the second tower).
4. Hatch also answers some of our notes as a draft titled "Re: [CC>MUSE] <our topic>" (signed H-7CH), not "[MUSE>CC]". Call list_drafts with query subject:"Re: [CC>MUSE]" newer_than:2d, view DRAFT_VIEW_METADATA_ONLY, pageSize 20, and search_threads with query subject:"Re: [CC>MUSE]" newer_than:2d -label:agent-notes-cc-processed, view THREAD_VIEW_METADATA_ONLY, pageSize 20. Our own replies put the tag first ("[CC>MUSE] Re: ..."), and Gmail's phrase search keeps word order, so these queries match Hatch's replies and not our notes.
5. Hatch sometimes files a reply with no tag at all (e.g. "Re: ScoutJob: ..."). It always signs "H-7CH". Call list_drafts with query "H-7CH" -subject:"[CC>MUSE]" -subject:"NCC-74656" newer_than:2d, view DRAFT_VIEW_METADATA_ONLY, pageSize 20, and search_threads with query "H-7CH" -subject:"[CC>MUSE]" -subject:"NCC-74656" newer_than:2d -label:agent-notes-cc-processed, view THREAD_VIEW_METADATA_ONLY, pageSize 20. The -subject:"[CC>MUSE]" part keeps our own notes out: some of them name H-7CH in the body, but every one of ours starts with the [CC>MUSE] tag (tested live 23 Oct: a probe note naming H-7CH was excluded). This query also returns the [MUSE>CC] and "Re: [CC>MUSE]" drafts already listed above; treat the union as one set. The -subject:"NCC-74656" part keeps Hatch's notes to the tower ("[MUSE>NCC-74656]") out of our set, so we never record them as ours (the sweep keeps anything naming NCC-74656 for 3 days).
6. Read /home/TurtleWolfe/.claude/state/agent-notes-processed.txt (one handled ID per line).

A note is NEW if it is a draft whose "id" is not in that file AND whose labelIds do not include Label_32, or a thread message whose labelIds do not include Label_32 and whose "id" is not in that file. (Since 20 Oct 2026 Google's Gmail MCP server refuses every message-level call on a draft, labels included, so a handled draft carries no Label_32: the ID in that file is the record. Never call a label tool on a draft.)

Your final line must be exactly one of:
NEW <space-separated ids>
NONE
